  "function": {
    "uniprot_function": "Casein kinase I isoform alpha is a serine/threonine protein kinase that regulates signal transduction and several core cellular processes. Functions in the Wnt signaling axis as part of the beta-catenin destruction complex. Phosphorylates CTNNB1 at Ser-45, priming it for subsequent sequential phosphorylation by GSK3A/GSK3B at Thr-41, Ser-37, and Ser-33, leading to ubiquitination by BTRC and proteasomal degradation. Also functions in circadian rhythm regulation by phosphorylating PER1 and PER2, stimulating their degradation and modulating circadian period length. Plays roles in p53-MDM2 regulation through phosphorylation cascades, nucleo-cytoplasmic shuttling of transcription factors, DNA repair, and DNA transcription. Substrate specificity: preferentially phosphorylates serine/threonine residues in canonical motif pS/T-X(n=2-4)-pS/T or noncanonical motif pS/T-X-pS/T, indicating preference for primed substrates.",
    "catalytic_activity": "ATP + [protein]-L-serine = ADP + [protein]-O-phospho-L-serine; ATP + [protein]-L-threonine = ADP + [protein]-O-phospho-L-threonine",
    "subcellular_location": ["Cytosol", "Nucleus", "Cytoskeleton", "Centrosome", "Nuclear speck", "Kinetochore fibers (during mitosis)"],
    "tissue_specificity": "Low tissue specificity with broad expression across tissues; cell type enhancement in keratinocytes",
